text:
{  
  "distractors": [  
    {  
      "text": "To help local shopkeepers increase their sales by promoting repairs instead of new purchases.",  
      "why_wrong": "This is incorrect because the text clearly states that the Repair Café has caused a drop in sales for some shopkeepers, like Ms. Whitmore. The café was not created to help businesses but to reduce waste and promote reuse."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "To provide free new appliances and clothing to residents who cannot afford them.",  
      "why_wrong": "This is false because the café does not give away new items; it helps people repair broken ones. The text emphasizes fixing old items, not replacing them with new ones."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "To replace the need for local shops by offering all household repairs in one place.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text never suggests the café aims to replace shops. In fact, it acknowledges shopkeepers’ concerns but frames the café as a community and environmental initiative, not a business competitor."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "To train young people in professional repair jobs for future careers.",  
      "why_wrong": "While skills are shared, the text does not mention career training or youth employment. The focus is on community engagement, waste reduction, and practical repairs, not job preparation."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "To generate profit for the town council through repair fees charged to residents.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text states the café is free and community-driven, with no mention of fees. It highlights savings in waste disposal costs, not income generation for the council."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "To replace the town’s recycling program with a system of in-person repairs.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text does not say the café replaces recycling; it complements environmental efforts by reducing waste through repair. There is no mention of replacing existing recycling programs."  
    }  
  ]  
}
